== Chapter VI

#align(center)[#block(width: 85%)[
  #set par(justify: false)
  #strong[The Inferences Drawn from the Proposition. A Use of Information.]
]]

#v(0.9em)

1\. #smallcaps[If] all things work for good, learn from this#footnote[#emph[Use] of Information.] that there is a providence. Things do not work by themselves; God sets them working for good.#footnote[1 #emph[Branch.]] God is the great disposer of all events and outcomes; he sets everything working. “His kingdom rules over all” (Ps 103:19); this is meant of his providential kingdom. Things in the world are not governed by #emph[second causes,] by the counsels of men, or by the stars and planets, but by divine providence. Providence is #emph[Regina mundi,] the queen and governess of the world. There are three things in providence: 1. πρόγνωσις, God’s #emph[foreknowing]\; 2. προορισμὸς, God’s #emph[determining]\; 3. διοίκησις, God’s #emph[directing] all things to their ends and outcomes. Whatever things work in the world, God sets them working. We read in the first chapter of #emph[Ezekiel] of #emph[wheels,] and #emph[eyes] in the wheels, and the #emph[moving] of the wheels. The wheels are the whole universe; the eyes in the wheels are God’s providence; the moving of the wheels is the hand of providence turning all things here below. What some call chance-medley, an accidental killing, is nothing else but the result of providence #footnote[1 Kgs 22:34.]

Learn to adore providence. Providence has an influence on all things here below. It is providence that mixes the ingredients and makes up the whole compound.

2\. It shows us the happy condition of every child of God: #emph[All things work for his good,] the #emph[best] and the #emph[worst] things: “Light dawns in the darkness for the upright” (Ps 112:4)#footnote[2 #emph[Branch.]]. The darkest, cloudiest providences of God have some sunshine in them #footnote[Τῶ δὲ θεοφιλοῦντι πάντα γίγνεσθαι τὰ ἄριστα δῆλον ἐστι. \[It is plain that for one who is dear to God all things turn out for the best.\] #emph[Plato.]]. What a blessed condition a true believer is in! When he dies he goes to God, and while he lives everything will do him good. Affliction is for his good. What hurt does the fire do to the gold? It only purifies it. What hurt does the fan do to the grain? It only separates the chaff from it. What hurt do leeches do to the body? They only suck out the bad blood. God never uses his #emph[staff] except to beat out the dust. Affliction does what the word often will not do: it #emph[opens the ear to correction] (Job 36:10). When God lays men on their backs, then they look up to heaven. God’s striking his people is like the musician striking the viol, which makes it give out a melodious sound. How much good comes to the saints by affliction! When they are pounded and broken, they give out their sweetest smell. Affliction is a bitter root, but it bears sweet fruit: “it yields a harvest of righteousness and peace” (Heb 12:11). Affliction is the highway to heaven; though it is flinty and thorny, yet it is the nearest way. Poverty will starve our sins; sickness will make grace more healthy #footnote[#emph[Languor corporis salutem animae parturit.] \[The sickness of the body brings forth the health of the soul.\]] (2 Cor 4:16). Reproach will cause “the Spirit of glory and of God” to rest on us (1 Pet 4:14): ἐγκλήματα, ἐγκωμία \[reproaches are praises\]. Death will stop the bottle of tears and open the gate of paradise. A believer’s dying day is his ascension day to glory. That is why the saints have put their afflictions in the inventory of their riches (Heb 11:26). #emph[Themistocles,] banished from his own country, afterward grew in favor with the king of #emph[Egypt,] and on this he said, #emph[Periissem, nisi periissem,] “I would have perished, if I had not perished.” So a child of God may say, If I had not been afflicted, I would have been destroyed; if my health and estate had not been lost, my soul would have been lost.

3.#footnote[3 #emph[Branch.]] See, then, what an encouragement there is here to become godly! #emph[All things will work for good.] Oh that this might tempt the world to fall in love with religion! Can there be a greater lodestone to piety? Can anything prevail with us more to be good than this, #emph[All things will work for our good]? Religion is the true philosopher’s stone, which turns everything into gold. Take the sourest part of religion, the #emph[suffering part,] and there is comfort in it. God sweetens sufferings with joy; he candies our wormwood with sugar. Oh how this may bribe us to godliness! “Acquaint yourself with Him and be at peace; thereby good will come to you” (Job 22:21). No man ever came away a loser by his acquaintance with God. #emph[Thereby good will come to you:] abundance of good, the sweet distillings of grace, the #emph[hidden manna,] yes, everything will work for good. Oh then get acquainted with God; take up his interest as your own.#footnote[4 #emph[Branch.]]

4\. It shows us the miserable condition of wicked men. To those who are godly, evil things work for good; to those who are evil, good things work for hurt: #emph[illis qui oderunt deum, etiam bona cedunt in malum] \[to those who hate God, even good things turn to evil\].

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[1\. #emph[Temporal good things] work for hurt to the wicked: riches and prosperity #footnote[#emph[In mundo splendor opum, gloriae majestas, amicitiarum presidia, verborum blanditiae voluptatum illecebrae ab amore dei homines abstrahunt.] \[In the world, the splendor of wealth, the majesty of glory, the protection of friendships, the flattery of words, and the enticements of pleasures draw men away from the love of God.\]]. They are not #emph[munera] \[gifts\] but #emph[insidiae] \[snares\], as #emph[Seneca] says. Worldly things are given to the wicked as #emph[Michal] was given to #emph[David,] for a snare (1 Sam 18:21). The vulture draws sickness from a perfume, and so do the wicked from the sweet perfume of prosperity. Their mercies are like poisoned bread given to dogs. Their tables are lavishly spread, but there is a hook under the bait: “May their table become a snare” (Ps 69:22). All their enjoyments are like #emph[Israel’s] quails, which were sauced with the wrath of God (Num 11:33).—#emph[Foenus pecuniae, funus animae] \[The interest of money is the funeral of the soul\].— Pride and luxury are the #emph[twins] of prosperity: “You grew fat … then he forsook God” (Deut 32:15) #footnote[#emph[Fulgentes voluptatis scintillulae praestigiis suis fascinant mentem, et πρὸς τὸ ἀκαλλὲς rapide impellunt.] \[The glittering sparks of pleasure bewitch the mind with their tricks, and drive it headlong toward what is ugly.\]]. Riches are not only the #emph[spider’s web,] unprofitable, but the #emph[cockatrice’s egg,] deadly: “wealth hoarded to the harm of its owner” (Eccl 5:13). #emph[Et transeunt, et vulnerant] \[They both pass away and wound\] #footnote[#emph[Ambrose.]]. The common mercies wicked men have are not lodestones to draw them nearer to God, but millstones to sink them deeper in hell (1 Tim 6:9). Their delicious dainties are like #emph[Haman’s banquet.] After all their lordly fare, death will bring in the bill, and they must pay it in hell.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[2\. #emph[Spiritual good things] work for hurt to the wicked; from the flower of heavenly blessings they suck poison.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[1\. The ministers of God work for their hurt. The same wind that blows one ship to the harbor blows another ship onto a rock. The same breath in the ministry that blows a godly man to heaven blows a profane sinner to hell. Those who come with the #emph[word of life] in their mouth are yet to many #emph[an odor of death]: “Make the hearts of this people calloused; deafen their ears” (Isa 6:10). The prophet was sent on a sad errand, to preach their funeral sermon. Wicked men are worse for preaching: they “hate the one who reproves in the gate” (Amos 5:10). Sinners grow more set in sin. Let God say what he will, they will do what they please: “As for the word you have spoken to us in the name of the LORD, we will not listen to you!” (Jer 44:16). The word preached is not #emph[healing] to them, but #emph[hardening.] And how dreadful this is, for men to be sunk into hell with sermons!]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[2\. Prayer works for their hurt: “The sacrifice of the wicked is detestable to the LORD” (Prov 15:8). A wicked man is in a great strait: if he does not pray, he sins; if he prays, he sins: “May his prayer be regarded as sin” (Ps 109:7). It would be a sad judgment if all the food a man ate turned to bad humors and bred diseases in the body. So it is with a wicked man: the prayer that should do him good works for his hurt. He prays against sin, and sins against his prayer. His duties are tainted with atheism and fly-blown with hypocrisy; God abhors them.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[3\. The Lord’s Supper works for their hurt: “You#super[+] cannot partake in the table of the Lord and the table of demons too. Are we trying to provoke the Lord to jealousy?” (1 Cor 10:21, 22). Some of the #emph[Jews] kept their idol feasts, yet would come to the Lord’s table. The apostle says, #emph[Are you#super[+] provoking the Lord to wrath?] Profane persons feast with their sins, yet will come to feast at the Lord’s table. This is to provoke God. To a sinner there is #emph[death in the cup]\; he “eats and drinks damnation on himself” (1 Cor 11:29). So the Lord’s Supper works for hurt to impenitent sinners. After #emph[the morsel, the devil enters.]]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[4\. Christ himself works for hurt to desperate sinners. He is λίθος προσκόμματος, “a stone of stumbling and a rock of offense” (1 Pet 2:8). He is so not of his own nature but only as the occasion of it, through the depravity of men’s hearts: instead of believing in him, they take offense at him. The sun, though pure and pleasant in its own nature, is hurtful to sore eyes. Jesus Christ is set for the #emph[fall] as well as the #emph[rising] of many (Luke 2:34). Sinners stumble at a Savior and pluck death from the tree of life. As chemical oils restore some patients but destroy others, so the blood of Christ, though to some it is healing, is to others damning. Here is the unparalleled misery of those who live and die in sin: the best things work for their hurt; cordials themselves kill.]]

#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[5\. See here the wisdom of God,#footnote[5 #emph[Branch.]] who can make the worst things imaginable turn to the good of the saints. By a divine chemistry he can extract gold out of dross. ὦ βάθος: “O, the depth of … the wisdom … of God!” (Rom 11:33). It is God’s great design to display the wonder of his wisdom. The Lord made #emph[Joseph’s] prison a step to promotion. There was no way for #emph[Jonah] to be saved but by being swallowed up. God allowed the #emph[Egyptians] to hate #emph[Israel] (Ps 106:41), and this was the means of their deliverance. St. #emph[Paul] was bound with a chain, and the chain that bound him was a means to spread the gospel (Phil 1:12). This wise physician can make an antidote out of the viper. God enriches by impoverishing; he increases grace by diminishing an estate. When the creature goes further from us, it is so that Christ may come nearer to us. God works in strange ways. He brings order out of confusion and harmony out of discord. He often makes use of unjust men to do what is just. #emph[He is wise in heart] (Job 9:4). He can reap his glory out of men’s fury#footnote[τὸ κλυδώνιον ἐιασε γενέσθαι καὶ βαπτισθῆναι τὸ σκάφος οὐκ ἐιασεν. \[He allowed the storm to arise, but did not allow the boat to be sunk.\]] (Ps 76:10). Either the wicked will not do the hurt they intend, or they will do the good they do not intend. God often helps when there is least hope, and saves his people in the way by which they think he will destroy them. He made use of the high priests’ malice and #emph[Judas’s] treason to redeem the world. God sometimes has much ado to please us. Through rash passion we are apt to find fault with things that happen, which is as if an unlearned man should criticize philosophy, or a blind man find fault with the work in a landscape: “Empty man would be wise” (Job 11:12). Silly creatures will be finding fault with providence and calling the wisdom of God to the bar of #emph[reason.] God’s ways are #emph[untraceable] (Rom 11:33). They are to be wondered at rather than fathomed. There is no providence of God that does not have either a #emph[mercy] or a #emph[wonder] in it. How astonishing and infinite is that wisdom that makes the most adverse and perplexing dealings work for the good of his children!]

6\. How little cause we have, then, to be discontented at outward trials and emergencies!#footnote[6 #emph[Branch.]] What? Discontented at what will do us good? #emph[All things will work for good.] There are no sins God’s people are more subject to than unbelief and impatience. They are ready either to #emph[faint] through unbelief or to #emph[fret] through impatience. When men break out against God in discontent and impatience, it is a sign that they do not believe this text. Discontent is an #emph[ungrateful] sin, because we have more mercies than afflictions; and it is an #emph[irrational] sin, because afflictions work for good. Discontent is a sin that pushes us into sin: “Do not fret yourself; it leads only to doing evil” (Ps 37:8). He who frets will be ready to do evil. Fretting #emph[Jonah] was sinning #emph[Jonah] (Jonah 4:9). The devil blows the coals of passion and discontent, and then warms himself at the fire. Oh let us not nourish this angry viper in our breast. Let this text breed patience: “All things work together for good to those who love God.” Shall we be discontented at what works for our good #footnote[μὴ ἀλγῶμεν ἐπὶ ταῖς θλίψεσιν, ἀλλὰ τὸ ἐκ τῶν θλίψεων κέρδος λογιζόμενοι, πάντα δεξιῶς φέρωμεν τὰ συμπίπτοντα. \[Let us not grieve at our afflictions, but, reckoning the gain that comes from them, let us bear well all that befalls us.\] #emph[Chrysost.]]? If a friend threw a bag of money at another man, and in throwing it broke his head, the man would not be much troubled, seeing that by this means he has got a bag of money. So the Lord may bruise us by afflictions, but it is to enrich us. These afflictions are working for us #emph[a weight of glory]\; and shall we be discontented?

7\. See here that scripture fulfilled: “Surely God is good to Israel” (Ps 73:1). #footnote[7 #emph[Branch.]] When a man looks on adverse providences, and sees the Lord covering his people with ashes and #emph[making them drunk with wormwood] (Lam 3:15), he would be ready to call the love of God into question, and to say that he has dealt harshly with his people. Oh no, #emph[yet God is good to Israel,] because he makes all things work for good. Is he not a good God who turns all to good? He works out sin and works in grace; is this not good? “When we are judged by the Lord, we are being disciplined so that we will not be condemned with the world” (1 Cor 11:32). The hell of affliction is to save us from the hell of damnation. Let us always justify God. However bad our outward condition is, let us say, #emph[Yet God is good.]

8\. See what cause the saints have to be frequent in the work of thanksgiving!#footnote[8 #emph[Branch.]] Christians are lacking in this: though they are much in petition, they are little in thanksgiving. The apostle says, ἐν παντὶ, “Give thanks in everything” (1 Thess 5:18). Why so? Because God makes everything work for our good. We will thank the physician though he gives us a bitter pill that makes us sick, because it is to make us well. We will thank any man who does us a good turn; and shall we not be thankful to God, who makes everything work for good to us? God loves a thankful Christian. #emph[Job] thanked God when he took everything away: “The LORD has taken away. Blessed be the name of the LORD” (Job 1:21). Many will thank God when he gives; #emph[Job] thanks him when he takes away, because he knew God would work good out of it. We read of saints with harps in their hands (Rev 14:2), an emblem of praise. We will meet many Christians who have tears in their eyes and complaints in their mouths, but few with harps in their hands, who praise God in affliction. To be thankful in affliction is a work peculiar to a saint. Every bird can sing in spring, but some birds will sing in the dead of winter. Almost everyone can be thankful in prosperity, but a true saint can be thankful in adversity. A good Christian will bless God not only at sunrise but at sunset. Well may we have a #emph[psalm] of thankfulness in the worst that befalls us, because all things work for good #footnote[τοῦτο φιλοσόφου ψυχῆς, ὑπὲρ ἁπάντων δοξάζειν, μὴ μόνον ὑπὲρ τῶν ἐυεργεσίων ἀλλὰ καὶ τιμωρίων. \[This is the mark of a wise soul: to give glory for all things, not only for benefits but also for punishments.\] #emph[Chrysost.] in Psal.]. Oh be much in blessing God. We will thank one who befriends us. —

9\. If the worst things work for good to a believer, what will the best things do, Christ and heaven? How much more will these work for good? If the cross has so much good in it,#footnote[9 #emph[Branch.]] what does the crown have? If such precious clusters grow in #emph[Golgotha,] how delicious is the fruit that grows in #emph[Canaan?] If there is any sweetness in the waters of #emph[Marah,] what is there in the wine of paradise? If God’s rod has honey at the end of it, what does his golden scepter have? If the bread of affliction tastes so good, what is #emph[manna?] What is the heavenly #emph[ambrosia?] If God’s blow and stroke work for good, what will the smiles of his face do? If #emph[grapes] may be gathered from #emph[thorns,] what fruit will the #emph[tree of life] yield? If temptations and sufferings have matter for joy in them, what will #emph[glory] have? If there is so much good out of evil, what then is that good where there will be no evil? If God’s #emph[chastening mercies] are so great, what will his #emph[crowning mercies] be? “Therefore comfort one another with these words.”

10\. If God makes all things turn to our good,#footnote[10 #emph[Bran.]] how right it is that we should make all things serve his glory! “Do it all to the glory of God” (1 Cor 10:31). The angels glorify God; they sing divine anthems of praise. How then ought man to glorify him, for whom God has done more than for the angels! He has honored us above them by uniting our nature with the Godhead. Christ has died for us, and not for the angels. The Lord has given to us not only out of the common stock of his bounty, but he has enriched us with covenant blessings; he has bestowed on us his Spirit. He takes care for our welfare; he makes everything work for our good. Free grace has laid a plan for our salvation. If God seeks our good, shall we not seek his glory?

#emph[Question.] How can we properly be said to glorify God? He is infinite in his perfections and can receive no increase from us.

#emph[Answer.] It is true that in a strict sense we cannot bring glory to God, but in an #emph[evangelical] sense we may. When we do all that lies in us to lift up God’s name in the world, and cause others to have high and reverent thoughts of God, the Lord counts this as glorifying him; just as a man is said to dishonor God when he causes the name of God to be spoken evil of.

#emph[Question.] In how many ways are we said to advance God’s glory? #emph[Answer.] In three ways. 1. When we aim at his glory#footnote[ὁ πάντα ποιῶν διὰ τὸν θεὸν πάντως ἀπολαύσεται τῆς ἀμοιβῆς· \[He who does all things for God’s sake will surely enjoy the reward.\]]\; when we make him the first in our thoughts and the last in our end. As all the rivers run into the sea, and all the lines meet in the center, so all our actions end and center in God. 2. We advance God’s glory by being fruitful in grace: “This is to My Father’s glory, that you#super[+] bear much fruit” (John 15:8). Barrenness brings dishonor on God. We glorify God when we grow in fairness like the lily, in tallness like the cedar, in fruitfulness like the vine. The spouse’s breasts were like “clusters of grapes” (Song 7:7).— 3. We glorify God when we give the praise and glory of all we do to God. It was an excellent and humble saying of the king of #emph[Sweden:] he feared that the people’s giving him the glory that was due to God would remove him before the work was done. When the silkworm weaves her intricate work, she hides herself under the silk and is not seen. When we have done our best, we must vanish in our own thoughts and transfer the glory of all to God: “I worked harder than all of them” (1 Cor 15:10). One would think this saying smacked of pride. But the apostle pulls the crown off his own head and sets it on the head of free grace: “yet not I, but the grace of God that was with me.” #emph[Constantine] used to write the name of Christ over his #emph[door,] and so should we over our #emph[duties.]

So let us endeavor to make the name of God glorious and renowned. If God seeks our good, let us seek his glory. If he makes all things serve our edification, let us make all things serve his exaltation. So much for the first part of the text, #emph[the privilege.]
